{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n`rebar fsck` has TWO different issue counts and they disagree.\n\n- **The exit code** comes from `_scan`'s counter, which respects each finding's `is_issue` flag:\n  `src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py` (`rc = 0 if issue_count == 0 else 1`), fed by\n  `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_tracker_health.py` (`issues += int(is_issue)`).\n- **`--output json`'s `issue_count`** is recomputed independently as `len(issues)` in\n  `_transform_json` (`src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py`), where `issues` is EVERY uppercase `KIND:`\n  line the text report emitted — with no knowledge of `is_issue`.\n\nSo the never-counted kinds inflate the JSON count while contributing nothing to the exit code.\nAdditionally, an uninitialised store renders in `--output json` byte-identically to a clean one.\n\n## Affected never-counted kinds (all pre-existing, is_issue=False)\n\n- `PUSH_PENDING` — `fsck_tracker_health.py` (informational sync-ahead)\n- `TRACKER_DIRTY_TMP_EVENT` — `fsck_tracker_health.py` `_DIRTY_LINE_SPECS`\n- `WARN:` lines — index-lock, forward-compat (`fsck_scan.py`), branch-mismatch\n  (`fsck_tracker_health.py`) → JSON kind `warn`\n- `STATUS_FORK_RESOLVED` — `fsck_scan.py`\n\n## Implementation plan (operator-approved — a THREE-SURFACE fix)\n\nAll three surfaces currently compute `len(issues)`; they must be changed consistently or they\ndisagree. `issue_count` is redefined to the COUNTED subset (respecting `is_issue`) so it AGREES\nwith the exit code; the field's TYPE is unchanged (integer, required). An ADDITIVE per-issue\n`counted` boolean is added so `issues[]` stays the complete findings list and a consumer wanting\nthe old total can still compute `len(issues)`.\n\n1. **`src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py`** (CLI JSON producer, `_transform_json`):\n   - Define the is_issue=False kind set WITHOUT a hand-maintained duplicate that can drift\n     from the per-site `is_issue` flags (plan-review G6). The tracker-dirty portion is\n     DERIVED from `_DIRTY_LINE_SPECS` — `_NEVER_COUNTED_TRACKER_DIRTY_KINDS = {kind for\n     (_, kind, _, counted) in _DIRTY_LINE_SPECS if not counted}` — so a future\n     `counted=False` dirty class flows in automatically. The three scan/health kinds decided\n     by an inline `is_issue=False` are an explicit `_NEVER_COUNTED_SCAN_KINDS =\n     {\"push_pending\", \"status_fork_resolved\", \"warn\"}`; each production site carries a\n     pointer comment back to the constant. `_NEVER_COUNTED_KINDS` is their union.\n   - Stamp each issue item with `counted = kind not in _NEVER_COUNTED_KINDS`.\n   - Set `issue_count = sum(1 for i in issues if i[\"counted\"])` — the counted subset, which\n     equals the exit-code tally.\n   - `_missing_tracker_result`: emit a synthetic `NOT_INITIALIZED:` finding (counted) in JSON\n     mode so the payload is distinguishable from a clean store and `issue_count` (1) agrees with\n     the exit code (1); still carry the tracker.dir mismatch `WARN` when present.\n\n2. **`src/rebar/_lib_reads.py`** (library facade, `_fsck_scan_report`):\n   - Update the `issue_count` fallback to count `counted` items instead of `len(issues)`.\n   - Keep failing closed for an unscannable store, now on the EXPLICIT `not_initialized` signal\n     (plus the existing empty-issues fallback), so `fsck_report()` still raises rather than\n     returning a fabricated report.\n\n3. **`src/rebar/_mcp_models.py`** (`FsckOut`): `issue_count: int` type unchanged; docstring\n   updated to state it is the counted subset and that `issues[]` items carry `counted`. Items\n   are `list[dict]`, so the additive `counted` flows through unchanged.\n\n4. **`src/rebar/schemas/fsck.schema.json`**: add an additive `counted` boolean property to the\n   issue item schema; update the `issue_count` and top-level descriptions to the counted-subset\n   meaning; note the `not_initialized` kind.\n\n5. **Docs**: `docs/user-guide.md` — make the \"never counted\" wording true of BOTH surfaces\n   (`counted: false` in `issues[]`, excluded from `issue_count`). Record the behavior change as a\n   BEHAVIOR CORRECTION / BREAKING entry in `docs/release-notes.md` AND `CHANGELOG.md` (field,\n   surfaces, migration for a consumer relying on the old count).\n\n6. **Tests**: update `tests/interfaces/facades/test_fsck_structured_326f.py` (drop the\n   `issue_count == len(issues)` pin, assert the counted semantics); add RED tests pinning each\n   never-counted kind → `counted=false` and excluded from `issue_count`, a counted kind →\n   `counted=true`, JSON `issue_count` agreeing with the exit-code tally on a mixed store, and the\n   uninitialised store distinguishable + `fsck_report()` still raising.\n   `tests/interfaces/store/test_fsck_ensures.py` already encodes the counted semantics and stays\n   green. `tests/interfaces/store/test_repair_pause_ownership_heldout.py`'s exact `abort` payload\n   pin gains the additive `counted: true` field.\n\n7. **Drift guard (plan-review G6)**: add tests in\n   `tests/interfaces/facades/test_fsck_issue_count_29c3.py` that keep `_NEVER_COUNTED_KINDS` in\n   lock-step with the per-site `is_issue` flags: a round-trip asserting the derived\n   tracker-dirty set equals `{kind for _DIRTY_LINE_SPECS if not counted}` (and that no\n   `counted=True` dirty class leaks in), that the constant is exactly the union of its two\n   documented components (no stray literal), and a behavioural invariant that the counted\n   subset equals the exit-code tally across a payload mixing every never-counted kind — so a\n   new is_issue=False kind that forgets the constant fails a test rather than silently\n   re-inflating `issue_count`.\n\n## Consumer assessment\n\nNo external or CI consumer reads `issue_count` (only rebar's own tests); this aligns the code\nwith `docs/user-guide.md`. It is a behavior change to a strong-contract field, recorded as\nBREAKING/behavior-correction in the release notes and changelog.\n\n**Reversibility (expand-contract / back-out).** The additive `counted` boolean is the *expand*\nhalf: it ships first and lets any consumer recompute the old total as `len(issues)` (or\n`len([i for i in issues if …])`) before any reliance on the redefined `issue_count`. The\nredefinition itself is fully reversible by reverting the three `len(issues)` → counted-subset\nedits (CLI `_transform_json`, `_lib_reads._fsck_scan_report`, and the schema/docs meaning); no\ndata migration is involved, so a back-out is a code revert. A consumer that must keep the old\nsemantics reads `len(issues)`; the `counted=false` items are exactly the difference.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `--output json`'s `issue_count` equals the COUNTED subset (respecting `is_issue`).\n- [ ] That `issue_count` AGREES with the fsck exit code on the same store.\n- [ ] The counted-subset meaning holds identically across all three surfaces: CLI, library\n      facade (`fsck_report`/`_fsck_scan_report`), and MCP (`FsckOut`).\n- [ ] `issues[]` keeps carrying every emitted finding (no loss of information).\n- [ ] Each `issues[]` item carries an additive `counted` boolean.\n- [ ] Every never-counted kind (`push_pending`, `status_fork_resolved`,\n      `tracker_dirty_tmp_event`, `warn`) is `counted=false` and excluded from `issue_count`, with\n      a test pinning each.\n- [ ] `_NEVER_COUNTED_KINDS` cannot silently drift from the per-site `is_issue` flags: the\n      tracker-dirty portion is derived from `_DIRTY_LINE_SPECS` and a drift-guard test fails if\n      the set diverges (plan-review G6).\n- [ ] An uninitialised store's JSON payload is distinguishable from a clean store and carries the\n      diagnostic.\n- [ ] `fsck_report()` still fails closed on an unscannable/uninitialised store.\n- [ ] `docs/user-guide.md`'s \"never counted\" wording is true of both surfaces.\n- [ ] The behavior change is recorded in `docs/release-notes.md` and `CHANGELOG.md`.\n\n## Discovered from\n\nBug `326f-595a-088b-40a5` (cutprice-untameable-stork), while making the MCP fsck tool return a\nstructured result."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787957029359196001,"uuid":"fdf99d4b-1fff-46a7-a1be-be80042a19dc"}